{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789820461,"nonce":"0iFunxw1HkD+lwi/UdL0A0fgmaU=","title":"Zyntax reload: only the module the runtime compiled last","message":"caribou_zyntax reloads a module through TieredRuntime::reload_typed_program, whose backend diffs the edited HIR against `current_module`, the single module the backend compiled last. Under caribou one runtime per language compiles every project module of that language in turn (game/scorer, then game/other), so a reload of anything but the last compiled module would diff against the wrong module: its functions would all count as \"added\", compiled fresh with new ids the runtime's function_ids map never learns, and the re-published interface would keep pointing at the old code. The adapter therefore refuses such a reload with an error naming the module compiled after it (State::current).\n\nAlso unhandled: a reload that adds a function (report.added) gets no entry in TieredRuntime::function_ids, so function_pointer(name) for it is None and the interface omits it until the next full load.\n\nLifting this needs a per-module reload context in zyntax's tiered backend (diff against the module the functions belong to, keyed by module name, and update function_ids for added functions), or one runtime per module on caribou's side, which is a Cranelift instance per module and was not chosen. Zyntax's own model is one program module per runtime plus library modules from snapshots; caribou's many-programs-per-runtime is the mismatch, and the same mismatch is behind bare function names sharing one function_ids namespace across modules.","files":null}]}